{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Silver, on his lips, for, cover, potter</b> — [verses 23–27] five joined together." "<b>Silver</b> — joined to 'dross' [in the construct state]." "<b>דולקים</b> [burning] — from the root דלק. And he mentioned silver, for לבת הדולקת [glazing that burns] is what resembles it. And the potter's vessel is compared to the thought of hatred. And so the interpretation: like silver refined from dross, overlaid upon a potter's vessel — so burning lips resemble silver. That is, just as silver is recognized when overlaid upon earthenware, so by his burning lips a hater is recognized. \n— n: 'Potter' here renders חרש/חרס; Ibn Ezra uses both spellings, referring to earthenware pottery. לבת is read as a glazing compound (cf. Akkadian lepû)."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:24:58.622244574Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}